Whisper: ask before an add-on starts a program (native messaging)

What: before Firefox starts the program of an add-on's native messaging host, it waits
for WhisperProtocolGuard.checkNativeMessaging, which asks the user with the add-on's
name, the host and the program's full path (or finds that the user already allowed
this add-on and host); if the user does not allow it, the program is not started and
the add-on gets the usual error.
Why: native messaging starts an ordinary program, outside the browser's sandbox, with
the user's rights, and Firefox asks nothing beyond the add-on's install-time
permission. Covers connectNative and sendNativeMessage (both go through this
constructor). The Flatpak/Snap portal path (Linux only) is not covered: there the
portal starts the program.
---
diff --git a/toolkit/components/extensions/NativeMessaging.sys.mjs b/toolkit/components/extensions/NativeMessaging.sys.mjs
--- a/toolkit/components/extensions/NativeMessaging.sys.mjs
+++ b/toolkit/components/extensions/NativeMessaging.sys.mjs
@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@
   AsyncShutdown: "resource://gre/modules/AsyncShutdown.sys.mjs",
   NativeManifests: "resource://gre/modules/NativeManifests.sys.mjs",
   Subprocess: "resource://gre/modules/Subprocess.sys.mjs",
+  WhisperProtocolGuard:
+    "moz-src:///browser/components/whisper/security/WhisperProtocolGuard.sys.mjs",
   maxRead: { pref: PREF_MAX_READ, default: MAX_READ },
   maxWrite: { pref: PREF_MAX_WRITE, default: MAX_WRITE },
   portal: {
@@ -103,7 +105,7 @@
       application,
       context
     )
-      .then(hostInfo => {
+      .then(async hostInfo => {
         if (!hostInfo) {
           this._throwGenericError(application);
         }
@@ -135,6 +137,14 @@
           disclaim: true,
         };
 
+        // Whisper: the user decides before the program starts (name, full path;
+        // WhisperProtocolGuard.checkNativeMessaging throws if not allowed).
+        await lazy.WhisperProtocolGuard.checkNativeMessaging(
+          context,
+          application,
+          hostInfo,
+          command
+        );
         return lazy.Subprocess.call(subprocessOpts);
       })
